<ProjectLayout
	title="ETF Tail Risk & Recovery"
	category="VaR · Survival Analysis · Quantitative Finance"
	year="2026"
	summary="A quantitative risk framework that studies both the magnitude of ETF downside losses and the duration of post-loss recovery."
>

	<!-- =====================================================
	     01 — RESEARCH QUESTION
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Research Question
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				The broader problem
			</p>

			<h2>
				How should downside risk be measured when both loss magnitude and recovery time matter?
			</h2>

			<p class="lead">
				Traditional Value at Risk summarizes how severe a loss
				may be over a given horizon, but it says little about
				what happens after that loss occurs.
			</p>

			<p>
				This project extends the risk question beyond the size
				of a downside event by also examining post-loss recovery
				as a duration problem.
			</p>

			<p>
				The framework combines rolling VaR estimation,
				backtesting, market-stress information and event-level
				recovery data to study ETF tail risk from both a
				magnitude and time perspective.
			</p>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     02 — WHY IT MATTERS
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Why It Matters
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<h2>
				A loss is not fully described by how large it is.
			</h2>

			<p>
				Two investments can experience losses of similar magnitude
				while exposing investors to very different recovery paths.
			</p>

			<p>
				One may recover quickly. Another may remain below its
				pre-loss level for an extended period.
			</p>

			<p>
				By combining tail-loss estimation with recovery duration,
				the project asks a broader risk-management question:
				not only <em>how much might be lost?</em>, but also
				<em>how long might the loss matter?</em>
			</p>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     03 — BIG QUESTIONS
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Big Questions
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				The analysis in four questions
			</p>

			<h2>
				Four questions define the framework.
			</h2>


			<div class="question-grid">

				<div class="question-card">

					<span class="question-number">
						01
					</span>

					<h3>
						How sensitive is estimated tail risk to the choice of VaR method?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Compare historical, parametric-normal and
						Monte Carlo VaR under the same rolling
						forecasting framework.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="question-card">

					<span class="question-number">
						02
					</span>

					<h3>
						Are VaR violations correctly calibrated and independently distributed?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Backtest whether exception rates align with
						the theoretical tail probability and whether
						violations cluster over time.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="question-card">

					<span class="question-number">
						03
					</span>

					<h3>
						How do market-stress conditions relate to downside events?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Incorporate VIX as a market-stress covariate
						and examine the environment surrounding
						VaR violations.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="question-card">

					<span class="question-number">
						04
					</span>

					<h3>
						How long does recovery take after an abnormal downside event?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Transform VaR violations into event-level
						duration observations that can support
						survival-based recovery analysis.
					</p>

				</div>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


