	<!-- =====================================================
	     06 — VaR MODELS
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			01 — VaR
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Tail-loss estimation
			</p>

			<h2>
				Three ways to estimate the same downside threshold.
			</h2>

			<p>
				Each method estimates a one-day-ahead VaR threshold
				using only information available before the realized
				return.
			</p>

			<p>
				Using the same rolling window makes differences between
				methods easier to interpret as differences in modeling
				assumptions rather than information availability.
			</p>


			<div class="method-grid">

				<div class="method-card">

					<span>
						Method 01
					</span>

					<h3>
						Historical VaR
					</h3>

					<p>
						Uses the empirical lower-tail quantile of
						recent observed returns without imposing a
						parametric return distribution.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="method-card">

					<span>
						Method 02
					</span>

					<h3>
						Parametric Normal VaR
					</h3>

					<p>
						Estimates tail risk using rolling mean and
						volatility under a Gaussian return assumption.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="method-card">

					<span>
						Method 03
					</span>

					<h3>
						Monte Carlo VaR
					</h3>

					<p>
						Simulates return outcomes from the rolling
						estimated distribution and uses the simulated
						lower-tail quantile.
					</p>

				</div>

			</div>


			<div class="definition-card">

				<span>
					Violation rule
				</span>

				<p>
					A VaR violation is recorded whenever the realized
					daily return falls below the forecast VaR threshold.
				</p>

			</div>


			<div class="project-tags">

				<span>Historical VaR</span>
				<span>Parametric VaR</span>
				<span>Monte Carlo</span>
				<span>Rolling Windows</span>
				<span>Tail Risk</span>
				<span>Forecasting</span>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     07 — BACKTESTING
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			02 — Backtesting
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Testing the forecasts
			</p>

			<h2>
				A VaR model should fail at the right frequency.
			</h2>

			<p>
				A risk forecast is useful only if its predicted tail
				probability is consistent with what subsequently occurs.
			</p>

			<p>
				The project therefore evaluates both the overall frequency
				of violations and whether those violations behave
				independently through time.
			</p>


			<div class="test-grid">

				<div class="test-card">

					<span>
						Test 01
					</span>

					<h3>
						Kupiec
					</h3>

					<p>
						Tests unconditional coverage: whether the observed
						VaR violation rate is consistent with the expected
						tail probability.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="test-card">

					<span>
						Test 02
					</span>

					<h3>
						Christoffersen
					</h3>

					<p>
						Extends the analysis by testing whether violations
						are independent rather than clustering during
						particular market regimes.
					</p>

				</div>

			</div>


			<div class="insight-card">

				<span>
					Why both?
				</span>

				<p>
					A model can produce approximately the correct total
					number of exceptions while still failing if those
					exceptions cluster during stressed periods.
				</p>

			</div>


			<div class="project-tags">

				<span>Kupiec Test</span>
				<span>Christoffersen Test</span>
				<span>Coverage</span>
				<span>Independence</span>
				<span>Model Validation</span>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     08 — RECOVERY
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			03 — Recovery
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Beyond loss magnitude
			</p>

			<h2>
				Treating recovery as a duration problem.
			</h2>

			<p>
				Once VaR violations are identified, those downside events
				can be reorganized into event-level observations for
				recovery analysis.
			</p>

			<p>
				This shifts the unit of analysis from daily returns to
				individual loss events: when the event began, the market
				conditions surrounding it and how long recovery takes.
			</p>


			<div class="recovery-flow">

				<div>

					<span>
						01
					</span>

					<strong>
						Identify breach
					</strong>

					<p>
						Locate dates where realized returns fall below
						the VaR threshold.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div>

					<span>
						02
					</span>

					<strong>
						Create event
					</strong>

					<p>
						Transform each violation into an event-level
						observation.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div>

					<span>
						03
					</span>

					<strong>
						Measure duration
					</strong>

					<p>
						Represent post-loss behavior through recovery time.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div>

					<span>
						04
					</span>

					<strong>
						Add stress context
					</strong>

					<p>
						Associate market-stress information such as VIX
						with the event.
					</p>

				</div>

			</div>


			<p class="note">
				The current repository prepares the event-level data and
				infrastructure required for survival-oriented recovery
				analysis rather than treating VaR as the final endpoint.
			</p>


			<div class="project-tags">

				<span>Survival Analysis</span>
				<span>Duration Data</span>
				<span>Recovery Time</span>
				<span>Event-Level Data</span>
				<span>VIX</span>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


